WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- So the question is,

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:04.820
can a new president revoke the
pardons of an old president?

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.200
I'm just, you know, curious
for no reason whatsoever.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.110
And the answer is it depends.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.790
Because it always depends.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:13.100
But we have some historical precedent.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.960
President Ulysses S. Grant
actually revoked several pardons

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.980
from former President Andrew Jackson.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.630
And the revocation actually went through

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.050
with respect to two men

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.180
because they were in prison at the time

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.100
and the warden hadn't actually given them

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.230
the physical document.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.570
It was almost literally on his desk.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.860
So you can revoke a pardon
that hasn't been delivered.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.050
But with respect to another man

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.930
that Grant tried to revoke the pardon of,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.210
he had already taken
possession of that pardon

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.000
and the court said that you can't revoke

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.090
that particular pardon.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.470
So if that precedent still holds,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:41.950
and that's always a question,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.010
then pardons that haven't
actually been given

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.320
to the pardonees can be revoked,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.000
but if they have been
delivered they can't.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.260
So I guess the lesson is work fast.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.490
Stay tuned for more
presidential legal news,

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.743
and as always, I'll see you in court.

